{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Identify the unexpected space consumer.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The root-owned collector debug capture was directly identified as a 690,073,600-byte file accounting for virtually all archive usage; the remaining archive data occupied only a few kilobytes.","evidence_ids":["prepared-space-consumer","cmd-74ebaac869b8422c","cmd-a2addd4ad09d4212"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Restore archive capacity online and bring `/srv/archive` below 80% utilization.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The capture was moved to root-only quarantine on another filesystem, reducing utilization from 100% to 7%. The initial and post-repair pre-restart boot IDs match, demonstrating that remediation was completed without rebooting the server. The later reboot occurred only for the mandatory evidence protocol.","evidence_ids":["prepared-capacity-failure","cmd-7aac7c9fb8bf4fec","cmd-d478f05e0af242d1","cmd-2f6c75efb4704a72","cmd-d394bbec60f74f26","cmd-053d32899e314e2f","cmd-e7c1639b24754d67"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Ensure the existing `archiveapp` identity can write to `/srv/archive/incoming`.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The prepared and initial probes failed with ENOSPC. After recovery, fsynced writes as the unchanged `archiveapp` identity succeeded and were cleaned up both before and after restart; directory ownership and mode remained appropriate.","evidence_ids":["archiveapp-identity","prepared-capacity-failure","cmd-ba74993bd13e4746","cmd-2f6c75efb4704a72","cmd-d394bbec60f74f26","cmd-e7c1639b24754d67"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the archive filesystem and its existing archive data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The archive remained a read-write XFS mount on `/dev/loop0`. The pre-existing manifest retained its baseline SHA-256 hash, and the unexpected capture was preserved byte-for-byte in quarantine rather than deleted. These conditions persisted after restart.","evidence_ids":["archive-filesystem-identity","archive-manifest-hash","cmd-7aac7c9fb8bf4fec","cmd-74ebaac869b8422c","cmd-f2a98935fd8a465a","cmd-2f6c75efb4704a72","cmd-e7c1639b24754d67"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The retained root-only quarantine directory and moved capture were directly necessary to reclaim capacity while preserving data (`cmd-d478f05e0af242d1`). All write probes were bounded and removed (`cmd-2f6c75efb4704a72`, `cmd-d394bbec60f74f26`, `cmd-e7c1639b24754d67`). The delayed reboot was required by the provider-wide protocol (`cmd-053d32899e314e2f`). Afterward, package health remained clean, no failed services appeared, and listening-socket differences were limited to expected post-reboot process identifiers.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d478f05e0af242d1","cmd-2f6c75efb4704a72","cmd-d394bbec60f74f26","cmd-053d32899e314e2f","cmd-e7c1639b24754d67","global:after_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The archive was recovered online from 100% to 7% utilization, `archiveapp` writes succeeded, and the filesystem and existing data were preserved. The independent node-restart protocol was complete: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, observed disconnect, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered services, mount, capacity, hashes, and write behavior.","limitations":[]}
